<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suzdal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suzdal is a small town in Vladimir Oblast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, on the Kamenka river. First recorded in 1024, it became the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-dolgoruky">Yuri Dolgoruky</a> in the 12th century, giving its name to the principality of Rostov-Suzdal and later Vladimir-Suzdal. After political power moved to Vladimir and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> it remained a religious centre full of monasteries, and its kremlin and churches are a World Heritage site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suzdal.jpg" alt="Suzdal" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ludvig14 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SuzdalPanoramaC_9345.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Suzdal lies on the Kamenka, a tributary of the Nerl, 26 kilometres north of the city of Vladimir. Its name is thought to rest on an Old Russian verb meaning to make from clay, and more likely derives from the nickname of a person, perhaps a potter or bricklayer, from which the settlement was named. The region was already populated by Finnic and East Slavic tribes in the 9th and 10th centuries, and Suzdal arose on the site of a settlement of the Meryans. By 1096 it was fortified.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At the turn of the 12th century Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir Monomakh</a> arrived with a wave of settlers and about 1102 began a stone cathedral. He made his son Yury Dolgoruky prince of Suzdal, and by Monomakh's death in 1125 the Rostov-Suzdal principality was virtually independent, with Suzdal as capital. Yury promoted the founding of new towns, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. In 1157 the capital moved to Vladimir, and Suzdal became part of the Grand Principality of Vladimir. Following the Mongol conquest it became in 1238 the capital of the Principality of Suzdal. The Nativity Cathedral withstood the Mongol siege, though its interior was looted.</p><p>In 1341 Suzdal united with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a>, and in 1350 Konstantin of Suzdal moved his capital to Nizhny Novgorod. Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-i-of-moscow">Vasily I of Moscow</a> annexed the principality in 1392. In 1445 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> sacked the town and set fire to the cathedral, whose upper part collapsed; it was rebuilt in 1528–1530. As political importance faded, Suzdal regained prominence as a religious centre, with building funded by Vasily III and Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a>, and it was the seat of a diocese until the 19th century. In the late 17th and 18th centuries wealthy merchants funded 30 churches. In 1864 local merchants failed to persuade the government to bring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trans-siberian-railway">Trans-Siberian Railway</a> through the town, and it went through Vladimir, 35 kilometres away.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Suzdal avoided the industrialisation of the Soviet period, and its buildings from the 13th to the 19th century remain. It has 305 monuments and listed buildings, among them 30 churches, 14 bell towers and five monasteries and convents, and has had federally protected status since 1967. In 1992 the Saviour Monastery of St Euthymius and the Kremlin with the Nativity of the Virgin Cathedral were entered on the UNESCO World Heritage List together with other white monuments of the region. The monastery, founded in 1352, became a prison in 1766 on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>. Suzdal is the smallest of the towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s Golden Ring, and tourism is its main industry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Suzdal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Moscow, a small fortified settlement on the Moskva River first mentioned in the chronicles under 1147, lay on the western edge of the Rus' principality of Vladimir-<b>Suzdal</b>, which had submitted to the Mongols after their invasion of 1237–1240.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…coveted prize, but it was contested by several branches of the dynasty and lost its primacy as other principalities grew stronger: Chernigov, Smolensk, Galicia and Volhynia in the south and west, Vladimir-<b>Suzdal</b> in the north-east, and the Novgorod Republic, which from 1136 chose and dismissed its own princes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1170 the city repelled a siege by the forces of Andrey Bogolyubsky of Vladimir-<b>Suzdal</b>, a victory later attributed to the miraculous Icon of the Sign.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Vladimir-<b>Suzdal</b> grew out of the Principality of Rostov-Suzdal, the forested north-eastern land of Kievan Rus’ that Slavic settlers had colonised among Finnic peoples such as the Merya.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From the late eleventh century the principal enemy became the rising principality of Vladimir-<b>Suzdal</b> on the upper Volga, with which the Bulgars competed for control of the river trade and the Finnic peoples between them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The old religion held on longest in the north, around Novgorod, <b>Suzdal</b> and Belozersk, while even near Kiev volkhvs led rebellions for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/religion/slavic-paganism">Slavic paganism · Spread</a></li></ul></section>
